const passages=[
{title:'A city library of objects',paragraphs:[
`A. In the fictional town of Bellford, a survey in 2018 found that many residents owned tools they used only once or twice a year. At the same time, other households postponed small repairs because buying equipment was expensive. The local library proposed lending objects alongside books. Its aim was to give people access to useful equipment without requiring every household to purchase and store its own collection. The proposal described access as the main goal; reducing waste was a possible additional benefit, not a result the library could promise in advance.`,
`B. The first collection contained sixty objects, including drills, sewing machines and basic gardening tools. Staff selected items using requests submitted by residents rather than a commercial supplier’s recommended package. They rejected equipment that required specialist supervision, even when several people had requested it. Small kitchen appliances were also excluded initially because the existing storage room lacked suitable cleaning facilities. This did not mean that such appliances were permanently prohibited: the manager said they might be considered if a different room became available.`,
`C. Borrowers needed an ordinary library card and had to attend a short safety introduction before taking a powered tool home. Borrowing was free, although a refundable deposit was required for the most valuable items. Loans lasted seven days and could be renewed once if nobody else had reserved the object. A reservation could be made online or by telephone. Staff deliberately retained the telephone option because some older residents had said that an online-only system would prevent them from participating.`,
`D. The pilot revealed an unexpected staffing problem. Checking an object on its return took longer than checking a book. A drill could appear intact while having a damaged cable; a sewing machine might be missing a small component that the next borrower would need. Staff created illustrated checklists and photographed the contents of each storage box. These measures reduced disagreements about missing parts. They did not eliminate the need for inspection, and the library continued to reserve staff time for checking every returned item.`,
`E. After six months, the library reported 420 loans. Gardening equipment was borrowed frequently in spring but rarely in winter, while sewing machines had a more even pattern of demand. A few popular objects developed long waiting lists. Rather than purchasing an identical replacement for every reserved item, the library examined whether demand was likely to continue. It bought a second sewing machine but delayed buying another hedge trimmer until the following spring. The manager argued that a short seasonal queue did not necessarily justify year-round storage costs.`,
`F. A follow-up questionnaire asked borrowers what they would have done without the service. Some said they would have bought a new object; others would have borrowed from a neighbour, hired equipment or abandoned the task. Consequently, the library did not equate each loan with a purchase avoided. Responses were voluntary and represented fewer than half of all borrowers. The report therefore treated estimated savings as uncertain. It also noted that the survey did not measure the energy used to transport equipment between homes and the library.`,
`G. The council agreed to extend the scheme for another year. Its decision drew on borrowing figures, user comments and evidence that the service reached households with limited storage space. The council did not claim that the project had already proved an environmental benefit. The next evaluation would compare transport patterns and maintenance costs as well as the number of loans. For the manager, the most useful lesson was that a practical service could succeed without every attractive claim about it being established. Good evaluation, she said, should make a successful project more understandable rather than merely make it sound impressive.`]},
{title:'When rivers have room to move',paragraphs:[
`A. In the invented Vale catchment, engineers once treated a fast-flowing river as evidence of efficient drainage. Channels were straightened and banks reinforced to move water away from farms as quickly as possible. These works reduced flooding in some fields. Yet water that left one place rapidly still had to pass through settlements downstream. During intense storms, several tributaries could deliver their highest flows at almost the same time. The catchment authority began asking whether the timing of water, as well as its total volume, deserved closer attention.`,
`B. A restoration trial reconnected one stretch of river to a floodplain that had been separated by an embankment. The project did not attempt to stop every flood. Instead, during high flows, water could spread over designated grassland and return to the channel more gradually. The land remained available for grazing in ordinary conditions. The authority negotiated compensation with participating landowners before construction began. Not all nearby farmers joined the trial, and the published report did not provide a single payment rate that applied to every agreement.`,
`C. Sensors recorded water levels upstream and downstream of the restored stretch. An adjacent, unrestored tributary supplied comparison data. The first two winters produced useful records but no storm as severe as the historical event that had flooded the town centre. The researchers observed a lower and later peak at the downstream sensor during several moderate events. They warned that these observations could not establish how the project would perform during every larger storm. A measure that works under one set of conditions may behave differently when the floodplain is already saturated.`,
`D. Another intervention involved planting trees along selected slopes. Roots and leaf litter can help water enter the soil, while vegetation can slow its movement across the surface. However, the team rejected the claim that planting any tree anywhere would provide the same benefit. Soil type, slope and existing drainage all mattered. The selected areas were mapped before planting. The team also avoided making immediate promises: young trees take time to establish, and the effects of a new planting scheme cannot be assumed to match those of a mature woodland.`,
`E. Public discussion often focused on photographs of water covering the grassland. Some residents interpreted these images as proof that restoration had made flooding worse. At a public meeting, engineers distinguished the planned flooding of an agreed storage area from the uncontrolled flooding of homes. They displayed a simple timeline showing when water entered and left different parts of the catchment. This did not resolve every objection, but it made the project’s purpose easier to discuss. The authority subsequently published sensor readings and plain-language explanations together.`,
`F. Maintenance presented a less visible challenge. Debris could block a culvert and change how water reached the floodplain. Fences damaged by high water needed repair, and invasive plants required monitoring. The project budget therefore included repeated inspections rather than construction costs alone. Local volunteers photographed a set of fixed observation points, but professional staff retained responsibility for safety decisions. The report did not suggest that enthusiastic volunteers could replace technical inspections. Their photographs instead helped staff notice changes between scheduled visits.`,
`G. The final recommendation described restoration as one part of a wider strategy. Flood warnings, building design and emergency planning remained necessary. The authority proposed expanding restoration cautiously while monitoring how separate measures interacted. Installing several interventions did not guarantee that their effects would simply add together. The study also recommended keeping records of unsuccessful events rather than publishing only favourable examples. The practical question was not whether natural processes or engineering should win a competition; it was which combination could reduce risk in this particular catchment, for whom, and under which conditions.`]},
{title:'The value of learning that feels difficult',paragraphs:[
`A. A smooth study session can be reassuring. Notes look familiar, examples seem obvious and answers arrive quickly. Yet a student’s experience during practice is not the same thing as what that student will remember next week. The distinction between present performance and durable learning has led researchers to examine activities that make practice less fluent. Some difficulties can support later recall, but difficulty itself is not a virtue. An unreadable page or an explanation full of unfamiliar terms may obstruct learning without producing any useful benefit.`,
`B. Consider two fictional classes learning the same set of scientific terms. One class rereads each definition several times. The other closes the book and attempts to recall a definition before checking it. During practice, rereading may feel easier because the information remains visible. Retrieval, by contrast, reveals gaps that a familiar-looking page can conceal. An unsuccessful attempt is not automatically helpful, however. Students need a reliable opportunity to compare their response with an accurate answer. Otherwise, they may leave the exercise uncertain about which parts of their explanation were correct.`,
`C. Timing changes the experience again. Practising all examples in one sitting can produce rapid improvement on that day. Distributing the same amount of practice over several days requires students to reconstruct information after some forgetting has occurred. The appropriate interval depends on the learner’s prior knowledge and the length of time for which the material needs to be retained. A fixed schedule may be convenient to organise, but it cannot be assumed to suit every learner and every goal. In particular, a student who cannot recall a basic idea may need support before a long delay is useful.`,
`D. Mixing problem types can create a different challenge. If ten examples all require the same procedure, identifying the procedure becomes almost unnecessary. In a mixed set, students must first decide what kind of problem they face. This can reduce the number of correct answers during the session while strengthening the ability to choose an approach later. Yet mixing is not an invitation to present unrelated material at random. Beginners may first need clear demonstrations of the individual procedures. The value lies in making a meaningful distinction, not in generating confusion for its own sake.`,
`E. Self-reports need equally careful interpretation. Learners sometimes judge an activity by how quickly it allows them to finish. A demanding retrieval exercise may therefore receive a lower enjoyment rating than rereading, even if later performance is stronger. Such a result would not make enjoyment irrelevant. If students avoid the exercise entirely, its theoretical benefit will have little practical value. Teachers can explain why an activity feels demanding, provide feedback and keep its workload manageable. These choices can help students persist without pretending that all productive practice should feel effortless.`,
`F. Adaptive software promises to adjust practice to each learner. A simple version brings a forgotten item back sooner and delays an item recalled accurately several times. But a correct click does not reveal everything about understanding. The learner may have guessed, recognised a visual cue or confused a word with a similar one. Response time and confidence can add information, although both are imperfect. A useful system should allow teachers to inspect decisions and override a schedule when illness, absence or a change of course makes the automatic plan inappropriate.`,
`G. Evaluating such a system requires more than counting completed cards. A platform may encourage frequent activity while doing little to improve transfer to unfamiliar tasks. Researchers therefore distinguish immediate scores, delayed retention and the use of knowledge in a new context. A vocabulary learner might recognise a translation but still struggle to use the word naturally in a conversation. No single measure captures all these outcomes. The strongest conclusion is modest: practice should be designed around the knowledge and actions learners need later, and its success should be tested against those goals rather than against the appearance of effortless progress.`]}
];
